# Tasks and partitions

A task type is code, shared across projects. A task row names that code (`code_key`) and stores this project's config. A partition is one batch of items. Labels hang off the item id, which is derived from the partition id and the locator. Changing a locator creates a new item and leaves the old label behind.

More work on an existing task is a new partition name. Rebuild of a partition is allowed only when the item ids stay the same. A different item set must be a new `--partition-name`. The head-present builder already exits in that case.

```bash
# ✅ GOOD — a new batch; old labels stay on pilot-100
python projects/voices/build_head_present.py --partition-name pilot-200 --n 100 --seed 2

# ❌ BAD — same name, different frames; this deletes the link to existing labels
python projects/voices/build_head_present.py --partition-name pilot-100 --n 100 --seed 9
```

Do not hard-delete a task, partition, or item. Archive it. New partitions start assigned to everyone. Narrowing the set is an assignment change, not a new partition.

A new task type needs all of these:

- A module under `backend/chorus/tasks/` with `code_key`, `code_version`, and the `TaskType` methods.
- A line in `backend/chorus/tasks/registry.py`.
- A viewer, a line in `frontend/src/tasks/registry.ts`, and an explicit branch in `Annotate.tsx`. Do not render the registry component dynamically.
- A project builder that writes the task, the partition, the items, and the manifest. Render stills on this machine. Do not bake overlays into the image.

SQL stays in the query modules. A new column is a new migration file. After a new partition, sync stills and the database the way `SYNC.md` says, and only after the current message allows that connection.
